export default {"builtAt":"2026-10-28T22:22:59.528Z","files":{"01-the-bet/diagnostic.md":"# Three-Axis Vulnerability Diagnostic\n\n> Week 1 · Oct 26, 2026 · Draft for partner challenge\n\n## Product\n\n**Product:** Leave Ledger. California leave of absence management for employers with 5 to 200 employees. It works out which state and federal leaves an employee qualifies for, what the state pays, and the notices and deadlines the employer owes, then gives HR, the employee and the manager each their own view.\n**Your Role:** Founder and product lead (solo build, test site on Netlify with 10 test accounts).\n\n**Scoring convention used here:** Contextual Moat and Data Advantage, 5 = strongest position. Platform Exposure, 5 = most exposed.\n\n---\n\n## The market in numbers\n\n| Segment | California businesses | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5 to 49 employees | 328,201 | Owe CFRA, PDL, bereavement and reproductive loss leave, but not federal FMLA. Most leave tools are built around FMLA. |\n| 50 to 249 employees | 48,240 | Owe all of the above plus FMLA. Where mid-market vendors start. |\n| **5 to 249 total** | **376,441** | Rough employee base: about 9.8M (midpoint estimate per size band) |\n\nSource: EDD size-of-business table, third quarter (statewide row). The employee figure multiplies each band's count by its midpoint, so treat it as an order of magnitude, not a fact.\n\n**Price anchor:** Tilt lists $4 to $6 per employee per month (G2, Dec 2025). At that price, 9.8M employees is a $470M to $700M a year ceiling. Real reachable revenue is a small fraction of that; M3 works the actual number.\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n### Contextual Moat · 2/5\n*Workflow depth × switching cost. Would users leave in a weekend if a competitor showed up?*\n\n**Score rationale:** The workflow is real: a deadline clock that starts when a manager hears about a leave, stacked entitlements (PDL then CFRA bonding, with SDI and PFL pay), notices, and three role views with server-side privacy. But today Leave Ledger holds no history and no documents, and it does not touch payroll. A user could move in a weekend. Switching cost appears once it holds the 12-month rolling CFRA history, signed notices and certifications for every employee, because that record is what an employer needs in a dispute.\n\n**Named attacker (draft, for partner challenge):** **Tilt** (formerly Sparrow). $4 to $6 per employee per month, two-way Gusto sync, all leave types nationwide. If Tilt adds a small-business tier, it arrives with payroll integration on day one, which Leave Ledger lacks.\n\n---\n\n### Data Advantage · 1/5\n*Proprietary signal that compounds with usage. What do you see that OpenAI doesn't?*\n\n**Score rationale:** No users yet, so no data. The rules engine encodes public law that anyone can read. The signal that could compound:\n1. How employees and managers actually describe a need for time off (\"my mom's surgery is next month\"), mapped to the leave type HR confirmed. This trains intake.\n2. Outcomes per California leave pattern: real durations by reason, how often PDL runs into bonding, how often certifications come back late.\n3. California edge cases HR had to override, which become rules or tests.\n\nNone of this exists until roughly 50 active employers are using it.\n\n**Named attacker (draft, for partner challenge):** **ChatGPT and other general assistants.** An office manager can already ask \"my employee is pregnant, what leave do they get in California?\" and get a decent answer for free. What the assistant lacks is the employee's hours, tenure, prior leave and the deadline clock. That is the gap to own.\n\n---\n\n### Platform Exposure · 4/5\n*Encroachment risk × pivot speed. If Apple/Google/OpenAI ships your hero feature native, then what?*\n\n**Score rationale:** For this product, the platforms are the payroll and HR systems small businesses already pay for. Encroachment is already happening, one tier up:\n- **Paylocity** acquired Aidora, an AI-native leave management company, on July 9, 2026. Aimed at mid-market and enterprise.\n- **Mitratech** launched Mitratech Leave, AI-native leave compliance, at SHRM26 in June 2026. Aimed at mid-sized and multi-state employers.\n- **Gusto, Rippling and Justworks** are where California small businesses run payroll. Any of them can bundle leave tracking at no extra price.\n\nPivot speed is the one strength: the codebase is small and could become an add-on inside a payroll marketplace within weeks.\n\n**Named attacker (draft, for partner challenge):** **Gusto.** It already holds the hours, hire dates and pay that eligibility depends on. A California leave module bundled into its plans would remove the reason to buy a separate tool.\n\n---\n\n## Top Vulnerability\n\nDistribution. Small businesses buy HR software through their payroll provider. If Gusto or Rippling ships California-specific leave (CFRA and PDL stacking plus a notice clock) before Leave Ledger has paying users, a standalone product has no way in.\n\n## Confidence Level\n\n**L (low).** The product logic is sound and the gap for 5 to 49 employee employers is real on paper, but nobody outside this build has used it or said they would pay. Moves to M after 10 interviews with California office managers or HR generalists.\n","01-the-bet/prototype.md":"# The Prototype Bet\n\n> Week 1 · Oct 26, 2026\n\n## What I Built\n\nA working test site where an HR admin, a manager and an employee each see a California leave from their side. The HR admin gets stacked entitlements, a state pay estimate, the notices owed, and a deadline clock. The employee gets their balances and next steps. The manager gets dates only.\n\n## Tool Used\n\nClaude (product, rules research, code), GitHub (`francis4531/leave-ledger`), Netlify (hosting, serverless API, Blobs storage). Cursor for quick local edits from here on.\n\n## Prototype Link\n\nhttps://spectacular-mousse-c413d6.netlify.app · 10 test accounts, shared password from Netlify settings.\n\nWhat works today:\n- **Rules engine** for CFRA, PDL, FMLA, SDI, PFL, paid sick leave, bereavement and reproductive loss, checked against sources in October 2026.\n- **Deadline clock**: 5 business days for the first notice, 15 calendar days for certification, 5 business days to designate. It skips weekends and company holidays.\n- **Server-side privacy**: managers never receive the reason for a leave, and employees only receive their own records.\n- **Manager flag**: when a manager hears about a possible leave, the notice clock starts.\n- **Copilot intake (added Oct 27)**: an employee describes the need in their own words (English or Spanish) and Claude Haiku 5.5 fills in the request form, marking anything it is unsure of. HR sees the original words next to what the Copilot read and what the employee changed. HR can also ask it to read a manager's note. The rules engine still decides eligibility. Every intake is logged with its corrections, and a 20-case golden set (`tests/intake-golden.json`, 4 adversarial) with `scripts/eval-intake.mjs` measures accuracy.\n\n## AI Value Archetype\n\n**Copilot** (confirmed Oct 26, 2026).\n\nThe prototype is a deterministic rules engine with the first Copilot piece (intake) on top of it. The AI layer sits on top of the rules engine, and the rules engine stays the only thing that decides eligibility:\n\n1. **Intake.** An employee or manager writes in plain words (\"my dad has surgery on the 12th, I need a few weeks\"). The copilot turns that into a draft case: likely leave type, family relation, dates. HR confirms it with one click.\n2. **Plain-language answers.** Employees ask \"will I get paid?\" and get an answer grounded in their own case, in English or Spanish.\n3. **Notice drafting.** The copilot fills in the eligibility, designation and return-to-work notices from the case for HR to review and send.\n\nThe copilot never decides eligibility or denies a leave; a human signs off on every notice. That boundary is the trust story for Module 4.\n\nWhy not the other archetypes: an **Automator** that sends notices on its own is too risky for legal notices. An **Oracle** with no workflow is what ChatGPT already gives away for free.\n\n## The Bet in One Sentence\n\nCalifornia employers with 5 to 200 employees get every leave right the first time: Leave Ledger turns \"I need time off\" into the right state and federal leaves, pay and deadlines, so a part-time office manager does not need an employment lawyer or a leave vendor built for 5,000-person companies.\n\n**Why now:**\n- CFRA reached employers with 5+ employees in 2021.\n- California keeps adding leave rules: bereavement (2023), reproductive loss and 40 hours of sick leave (2024), and PFL for a designated individual from July 2028.\n- The AI-native leave tools that launched in 2026 (Aidora, now part of Paylocity; Mitratech Leave) are built for mid-market and enterprise.\n\n## Kill Criteria\n\nStop, or pivot to an add-on inside a payroll platform, if any of these happen:\n\n| # | Evidence that kills or changes the bet | Check by |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Fewer than 3 of 10 interviewed California office managers or HR generalists handled a CFRA or PDL leave in the last 12 months and called it painful | Nov 23, 2026 |\n| 2 | Fewer than 2 of those 10 would pay at least $4 per employee per month (Tilt's lowest tier) or a flat monthly price of similar size | Nov 23, 2026 |\n| 3 | Gusto or Rippling ships California-specific leave stacking with a notice clock before Leave Ledger has 5 paying employers | Ongoing; review monthly |\n| 4 | AI intake classifies under 90% of a 50-case golden set into the right leave type | Built and tested in Module 4 |\n\nIf #4 fails, the bet survives as a rules-and-workflow product with no AI in the decision path, and the archetype is revisited.\n","02-the-moat/data-flywheel.md":"# Data Flywheel Map\n\n> Week 2 · Oct 28, 2026 · Draft for partner challenge\n> Score each loop 1-5. Your weakest loop is where competitors attack first.\n\n**Starting point:** Leave Ledger is a test build with 10 test accounts and no real customers. The Copilot intake (built Oct 27) is the first part that produces data that could compound. Scores below describe what exists today, not the plan.\n\n## Flywheel Loops\n\n| Loop | What It Measures | Score 1 | Score 5 | Score |\n|------|------------------|---------|---------|-------|\n| **Correction** | Do users fix AI outputs? Is that signal captured and reused? | No capture | Automated retraining | 2/5 |\n| **Preference** | Does the product learn individual / team preferences over time? | Stateless | Deep personalization | 1/5 |\n| **Domain Context** | Does usage in one area improve quality in adjacent areas? | Siloed | Cross-domain transfer | 2/5 |\n| **Network** | Does each new user / team make the product better for everyone? | Isolated | Strong network effects | 1/5 |\n\n### Correction Loop · 2/5\n**What you capture today:** Every Copilot intake is stored with the user's own words, what the Copilot read (leave type, family member, start date, length, confidence per field) and which fields the user changed before sending. HR sees \"Sent as the Copilot read it\" or \"Changed before sending: start date\". HR Settings shows the share sent without changes and the count of fixes per field.\n**How it compounds:** It doesn't yet. Corrections are captured but nothing feeds them back. Step to 3/5: a weekly review that turns each fixed intake into a row in `tests/intake-golden.json`, and the most common misses into prompt examples, with the eval score tracked over time. Step to 4/5: per-company examples (\"at this company, 'a few weeks' usually means 4\").\n\n### Preference Loop · 1/5\n**What you capture today:** Nothing per company or per user beyond settings (headcount, holidays). The product does not know whether a company pays for bereavement, tops up state pay, or lets employees use vacation first.\n**How it compounds:** A company policy profile, learned from HR's edits and set once in Settings, would change the plan and the notices for every future leave at that company. Employee language preference (Spanish) would carry into answers and notices. Each is small, but together they make switching costly: a new tool starts without them.\n\n### Domain Context Loop · 2/5\n**What you capture today:** One case feeds the next for the same employee. CFRA weeks used in the rolling 12 months reduce what is left, sick hours carry across requests, and the manager's flag becomes the HR case. The rules engine is shared across all 8 leave types, so a rule fix (for example, holidays in the deadline count) improves every leave.\n**How it compounds:** Within a company, a full history makes each new decision more accurate (no double-counting of CFRA, no missed PDL time). This is the start of switching cost: a competitor would not have the 12-month record that a dispute depends on.\n\n### Network Loop · 1/5\n**What you capture today:** Nothing. Each company is isolated, which is correct for health-related data.\n**How it compounds:** Only through de-identified, aggregated benchmarks shown to HR: typical length by leave type, how often certifications come back late, how often PDL runs into bonding. Two limits: it needs enough companies to be meaningful and safe (for example, no figure shown unless it pools at least 10 companies), and leave reasons are sensitive health information, so it must be opt-in and never expose an individual. A second network effect is a shared library of California edge cases (rules HR had to override), which helps every customer and holds no personal data.\n\n**Total Flywheel Score: 6/20**\n**Weakest Loop:** Network and Preference (1/5 each). Network is the one a competitor with scale attacks first: Tilt, Gusto or Paylocity see far more leaves than Leave Ledger will for years.\n**Fix for weakest loop:** Do not try to win on network effects. Invest in Correction and Preference, which work with a single customer, and build the de-identified California benchmark only after 25 or more active companies. The near-term fix is the Correction loop (weekly golden-set review), because it costs nothing and its score is already measured.\n\n---\n\n## Competitive Position\n\nTwo axes that matter to a 5 to 49 employee California employer:\n\n- **California depth** (horizontal): from generic multi-state leave tracking to full California stacking (PDL then CFRA bonding, SDI and PFL pay, bereavement, reproductive loss, notice clock).\n- **Fit for a small business** (vertical): from enterprise sales and implementation to self-serve setup at a price a 20-person company accepts.\n\n| Player | California depth | Small-business fit | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Paylocity + Aidora | High | Low | AI-native leave, acquired Jul 2026; aimed at mid-market and enterprise |\n| Mitratech Leave | High | Low | AI-native leave compliance, launched Jun 2026; mid-sized and multi-state |\n| Tilt | Medium to high | Medium | $4 to $6 per employee per month, in-house leave experts, Gusto and Rippling integrations, AI assistant \"Tilley\" with English and Spanish phone intake (premium support customers) |\n| Rippling Leave Management | Medium | Medium | Built-in leave app; Rippling suggests adding Tilt for complex cases |\n| Gusto | Low | High | PTO requests, accruals and balances inside payroll; no sign of CFRA eligibility, notices or state leave stacking |\n| CalChamber | High (content) | High | Forms, guides and a helpline, not a workflow |\n| ChatGPT and similar | Medium | High | Free answers; no employee data, no deadlines, no record |\n| **Leave Ledger** | **High** | **High (target)** | Rules engine, notice clock, three role views, Copilot intake; no integrations, no customers |\n\n**Placement:** Leave Ledger aims for the top right corner, where only CalChamber (content, not software) and Tilt (partly) sit today. That corner is attractive and also the easiest to copy, because the rules are public law.\n\nSources: [Tilt's Tilley assistant](https://hellotilt.com/platform/tilley/), [Rippling and Tilt](https://www.rippling.com/blog/tilt-rippling-the-easy-way-to-automate-leave-management), [Gusto time off, per LeaveBalance (a competitor), Jul 2026](https://www.leavebalance.com/blog/gusto-time-off-tracking/), [Tilt pricing on G2](https://www.g2.com/products/tilt-tilt/pricing). M1 sources cover Paylocity and Mitratech.\n\n---\n\n## Encroachment Threat Assessment\n\nTime-to-threat and value-at-risk figures are estimates for partner challenge, not measurements.\n\n### 1. Platform Encroachment\n**Attacker:** Gusto (where California small businesses already run payroll).\n**Vector:** Adds a California leave module to its plans: CFRA and PDL eligibility from the hours and hire dates it already holds, a notice checklist, and payroll pay codes that update automatically.\n**Time-to-threat:** 6 to 18 months. Gusto has the data and the customers; nothing public suggests it is building this now.\n**% of value at risk:** About 70%. Eligibility, balances and tracking would be covered. What it would likely not match early: the notice clock tied to the manager flag, PDL to CFRA stacking detail, and the Copilot.\n\n### 2. Vertical Competitor\n**Attacker:** Tilt.\n**Vector:** A lower-priced, self-serve tier for small employers, using its existing Gusto integration and Tilley assistant.\n**Time-to-threat:** 3 to 12 months. It already has the product, the AI intake and the integrations; the only gap is packaging for very small companies.\n**% of value at risk:** About 80%. Tilt's main weakness here is price and a sales-led model built for larger companies.\n\n### 3. Adjacent Expansion\n**Attacker:** CalChamber.\n**Vector:** Turns its leave content, forms and helpline into a guided workflow for its existing California members.\n**Time-to-threat:** 12 to 24 months. Its strength is trust and distribution, not software.\n**% of value at risk:** About 40%, mostly the \"help me get this right\" value for 5 to 49 employee members.\n\n---\n\n## 90-Day Encroachment Plan\n\n*Draft attack for your partner to play and sharpen.*\n\n**Attacker:** Gusto.\n**Attack vector (target the weakest loop):** Network. Gusto sees leave patterns across hundreds of thousands of small businesses. It ships benchmarks and defaults (\"most California companies your size give 5 paid bereavement days\") that Leave Ledger cannot match.\n**Weeks 1-4 - what they ship:** \"California leave\" inside Gusto: an eligibility check (CFRA, PDL) from payroll data, a leave request type, and a notice checklist, free in existing plans.\n**Weeks 5-8 - how they poach users:** An in-app banner for every California customer with 5 or more employees: \"You owe CFRA leave. We now track it for you.\" One-click setup, no new vendor, no new login, no per-employee fee.\n**Weeks 9-12 - why users don't come back:** Leave pay codes, balances and history now live in payroll. Leaving Gusto's module means re-entering history and losing automatic pay updates.\n**Your defense:**\n1. **Be inside Gusto, not against it.** List in Gusto's app directory with a two-way sync (hours, hire dates, pay codes). Position as the California leave specialist that Gusto customers add, the way Tilt already does.\n2. **Own the record.** The notice clock, the signed notices and the 12-month leave history are what an employer needs in a dispute. Make exporting it easy, and make keeping it in Leave Ledger the safer choice.\n3. **Win on correctness for California.** Publish the rules engine's test cases (PDL to bonding, bereavement within 3 months, reproductive loss caps) and the Copilot's accuracy score. A free bundled checklist does not ship with either.\n4. **Go through advisors.** Payroll bookkeepers, PEOs and employment lawyers who serve 5 to 49 employee companies are a channel Gusto does not control.\n","02-the-moat/kill-switch.md":"# Kill Switch Audit\n\n> Week 2 · Oct 28, 2026 · Draft for partner challenge\n\n**Where AI is used today:** only the Copilot intake (`netlify/lib/intake.mjs`). One model call per request, about 1,500 tokens in and 200 out. Eligibility, pay estimates and deadlines run in the rules engine with no AI. If the AI is off or failing, users fill in the form by hand and nothing else changes.\n\n## Vendor Dependency Assessment\n\n| Dimension | Current State | Risk Level | 48-Hour Action |\n|-----------|--------------|------------|---------------|\n| **Provider** | One provider: Anthropic, Claude Haiku 5.5, called directly over its API. Model name set by `LL_MODEL`. | M | Run the 20-case golden set on a second provider's small model and record the score. |\n| **Abstraction** | Partial. The prompt and the output contract (a JSON draft validated field by field) are provider-neutral, but the HTTP call is written for Anthropic's API. | L | Move the call behind a small adapter (`callModel(system, message)`) with Anthropic and one other provider. |\n| **Routing** | None. Every request goes to one model. There is no fallback model, only the manual form. | M | Add a fallback: if the main model errors or times out, retry once on the second provider; if that fails, show the form. |\n| **Eval** | A 20-case golden set (4 adversarial) and `scripts/eval-intake.mjs` exist, plus a live \"sent without changes\" rate in HR Settings. Not run against the real model yet. | M | Add the API key, run the eval, and record the first baseline score in this file. |\n\n## Portability Score\n\n**Partial.** The parts that make the product valuable (rules engine, deadline clock, role views, the intake output contract) do not depend on any AI vendor. Switching models means changing one file and re-running the golden set. It is not \"Ready\" until a second provider is wired in and scored.\n\n## If Anthropic doubles pricing tomorrow:\n\nLittle changes. At today's list price (Haiku 5.5: $0.10 per million input tokens, $0.50 per million output) one intake costs about $0.00025. Doubled, about $0.0005. A 20-employee company with 10 leaves a year would spend under one cent a year on intake. The 48-hour response is to do nothing on cost, and use the moment to finish the adapter and second-provider eval above.\n\nSource: [Claude Haiku 5.5 overview and pricing](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/haiku-5-5/overview). Per-request tokens are an estimate from the prompt size; the live number will appear in HR Settings.\n\n## If Anthropic ships a competing product:\n\nA general assistant that answers California leave questions does not threaten Leave Ledger more than ChatGPT already does. What a model vendor cannot replicate without becoming an HR software company:\n1. **The employer's own data.** Hours, hire dates, 12-month leave history and sick balances per employee.\n2. **The deadline clock and the record.** Notices owed, when the clock started (including from a manager's flag), what was sent and when.\n3. **Role-based privacy.** Managers see dates only, employees see their own case, HR sees everything.\n4. **Distribution into payroll** (the M2 defense), once built.\n\nThe real vendor risk is not competition; it is a model change that quietly lowers intake accuracy. The golden set run on every model change is the defense.\n","03-the-margin/cost-curve.md":"# Cost Curve & Pricing Strategy\n\n## Cost Model\n\n| Cost Category | Per-User/Month | Notes |\n|--------------|----------------|-------|\n| Inference (primary model) | | |\n| Inference (cascading/triage) | | |\n| Infrastructure | | |\n| Data/storage | | |\n| Human-in-the-loop | | |\n| **Total AI COGS** | | |\n\n## Cascading Strategy\n<!-- Cheap model → frontier model routing logic -->\n\n**Triage model:**\n**Frontier model:**\n**Routing rule:**\n**Expected cascade ratio:**\n\n## Pricing Model\n\n**Current pricing:**\n**Proposed AI pricing:**\n**Model:** seat-based / usage-based / outcome-based / hybrid\n\n## Stress Tests\n\n| Scenario | Impact on Margin | Response |\n|----------|-----------------|----------|\n| Inference costs 3x | | |\n| Heaviest segment doubles | | |\n| Model provider raises prices 50% | | |\n\n## Board One-Pager\n<!-- Before/After: Old SaaS revenue vs. AI usage revenue for your product -->\n\n**Before (traditional SaaS):**\n**After (AI-enabled):**\n**Net margin shift:**\n","04-the-contract/golden-dataset.md":"# Golden Dataset & Reliability Contract\n\n## Golden Dataset Spec\n\n| # | Input | Expected Output | Edge Case? | Judge Type |\n|---|-------|----------------|-----------|-----------|\n| 1 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n| 2 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n| 3 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n| 4 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n| 5 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n\n**Adversarial rows included:** __\n**Coverage gaps identified by partner:**\n\n## Confidence UX Design\n\n**Approach:** show uncertainty / tiered confidence / human-in-loop trigger\n\n**High confidence (>90%):**\n**Medium confidence (70-90%):**\n**Low confidence (<70%):**\n\n**User control surface:**\n\n## Reliability Contract\n\n| Metric | Target | Measurement | Alert Threshold |\n|--------|--------|-------------|-----------------|\n| Accuracy | | | |\n| Hallucination rate | | | |\n| Latency (p95) | | | |\n| Drift velocity | | | |\n\n## HITL Architecture\n<!-- When does a human step in? What's the escalation path? -->\n\n## Red-Team Findings\n*What failure mode did your partner find that you missed?*\n","05-the-guardrails/compounding-system.md":"# Compounding System Design\n\n## Feedback Loops\n\n| Loop | Input | Output | Compounds? | Status |\n|------|-------|--------|-----------|--------|\n| | | | Y/N | active / broken / missing |\n| | | | Y/N | active / broken / missing |\n| | | | Y/N | active / broken / missing |\n\n**Broken loop identified by partner:**\n**Fix plan:**\n\n## Context Connectivity\n<!-- How does knowledge flow across teams and domains? Where does it silo? -->\n\n## Governance Policy\n\n**Scope:**\n**Autonomy boundaries:**\n**Escalation triggers:**\n**Audit cadence:**\n**Regulatory exposure (EU AI Act / other):**\n\n## Agent Topology\n<!-- If using agents: what can each agent do? What can't it do? Who approves what? -->\n\n## Shadow AI Audit\n\n| Tool | Owner | Risk Level | Decision |\n|------|-------|-----------|----------|\n| | | H / M / L | keep / govern / kill |\n| | | H / M / L | keep / govern / kill |\n| | | H / M / L | keep / govern / kill |\n\n**Total tools found:**\n**Tools after triage:**\n**Estimated hidden spend:**\n","06-the-pitch/roadmap.md":"# Three-Horizon Roadmap & Board Pitch\n\n## Roadmap\n\n### Horizon 1 · Now (0-3 months)\n*Quick wins. Ship with existing capabilities.*\n\n| Initiative | Metric | Confidence |\n|-----------|--------|-----------|\n| | | H / M / L |\n| | | H / M / L |\n\n### Horizon 2 · Next (3-9 months)\n*Bets. Requires new capabilities or integrations.*\n\n| Initiative | Metric | Confidence |\n|-----------|--------|-----------|\n| | | H / M / L |\n| | | H / M / L |\n\n### Horizon 3 · Bet (9-18 months)\n*Moonshots. High uncertainty, high potential.*\n\n| Initiative | Metric | Confidence |\n|-----------|--------|-----------|\n| | | H / M / L |\n\n## Board Pitch\n\n**Thesis (1 sentence):**\n\n**The case:**\n1. Why now:\n2. What's defensible:\n3. The economics:\n\n**The risks:**\n1. Trust / failure modes:\n2. Scale / governance:\n3. Competitive:\n\n**The ask:**\n\n## M1 Baseline vs. Now\n*Your 3-sentence AI strategy from Module 1 vs. what you'd say now:*\n\n**M1 baseline:**\n\n**Now:**\n","README.md":"# Leave Ledger · AI Product Strategy\n\n> A living strategy built across 6 weekly sessions, using the Product School AI product strategy template. Each week adds one module. By week 6, this folder is the strategy: version-controlled and ready for a board.\n\n**The bet:** California leave of absence management for employers with 5 to 200 employees.\n**Last updated:** Oct 28, 2026 (Week 2)\n**Customer interviews:** 0 of 10 (kill criteria check on Nov 23, 2026)\n**Partner challenges done:** 0 of 6\n\n---\n\n## Strategy at a Glance\n\n| Component | Module | Week of | Status | Key Artifact |\n|-----------|--------|---------|--------|-------------|\n| **The Bet** | M1 | Oct 21 | [x] Draft, needs partner challenge | `01-the-bet/` |\n| **The Moat** | M2 | Oct 28 | [x] Draft, needs partner challenge | `02-the-moat/` |\n| **The Margin** | M3 | Nov 4 | [ ] | `03-the-margin/` |\n| **The Contract** | M4 | Nov 11 | [ ] | `04-the-contract/` |\n| **The Guardrails** | M5 | Nov 18 | [ ] | `05-the-guardrails/` |\n| **The Pitch** | M6 | Nov 25 | [ ] | `06-the-pitch/` |\n\n---\n\n## The Bet (M1)\n\n**What we're building, for whom, why now.**\n\n- **Product:** Leave Ledger. Works out which California and federal leaves an employee qualifies for, what the state pays, and the notices and deadlines the employer owes. Separate views for HR, employee and manager.\n- **AI Value Archetype:** Copilot (confirmed Oct 26). AI handles intake, plain-language answers and notice drafts; a deterministic rules engine decides eligibility; a human signs off on every notice.\n- **Vulnerability Scores:** Moat 2/5 · Data 1/5 · Platform 4/5 (exposed)\n- **Top Risk:** Distribution. Small businesses buy HR through their payroll provider. If Gusto or Rippling ships California leave first, a standalone tool has no way in.\n- **Confidence:** L (until 10 customer interviews)\n- **Prototype:** Netlify test site, 10 test accounts (link in `01-the-bet/prototype.md`)\n- **Kill Criteria:** Under 3 of 10 interviewees feel the pain, or under 2 of 10 would pay $4 per employee per month, by Nov 23; or a payroll platform ships California leave stacking before we have 5 paying employers.\n\n→ Details: [`01-the-bet/`](01-the-bet/)\n\n---\n\n## The Moat (M2) · Week of Oct 28\n\n**Why this won't get copied in 6 months.**\n\n- **Data Flywheel Score:** 6/20 (Correction 2 · Preference 1 · Domain Context 2 · Network 1)\n- **Weakest Loop:** Network. Gusto, Tilt and Paylocity see far more leaves than we will for years. Fix: don't compete there; build Correction (weekly golden-set review of Copilot fixes) and Preference (company policy profile) first; add de-identified California benchmarks only after 25+ companies, opt-in, never below 10 pooled companies.\n- **Competitive Position:** Axes are California depth and fit for a 5 to 49 employee business. Leave Ledger targets high on both. Paylocity+Aidora and Mitratech are deep but enterprise; Gusto fits small business but is shallow on California leave; Tilt is closest (AI phone intake, Gusto and Rippling integrations, $4 to $6 per employee per month).\n- **Encroachment Defense:** Join Gusto rather than fight it (app directory listing with two-way sync); own the dispute-ready record (notice clock, signed notices, 12-month history); publish rules-engine tests and the Copilot accuracy score; sell through bookkeepers, PEOs and employment lawyers.\n- **Vendor Portability:** Partial. One AI provider and one call site; the rules engine has no AI dependency. 48-hour fix: adapter plus a second provider scored on the golden set.\n\n→ Details: [`02-the-moat/`](02-the-moat/)\n\n---\n\n## The Margin (M3) · Week of Nov 4\n\n**Will this make money or bleed it?**\n\n- **Gross Margin (current):**\n- **Gross Margin (AI-adjusted):**\n- **Pricing Model:**\n- **Cascading Strategy:**\n- **Break-even at:**\n\n→ Details: [`03-the-margin/`](03-the-margin/)\n\n---\n\n## The Contract (M4) · Week of Nov 11\n\n**Why users will trust a probabilistic system.**\n\n- **Reliability Target:**\n- **Golden Dataset:** __ rows, __ adversarial\n- **Confidence UX:** [approach]\n- **HITL Architecture:**\n- **Failure Mode Coverage:**\n\n→ Details: [`04-the-contract/`](04-the-contract/)\n\n---\n\n## The Guardrails (M5) · Week of Nov 18\n\n**What breaks when this scales, and what compounds.**\n\n- **Compounding System:** [describe feedback loops]\n- **Governance Posture:** [approach]\n- **Shadow AI Status:** __ tools found, __ triaged\n- **Agent Boundaries:**\n- **Regulatory Exposure:**\n\n→ Details: [`05-the-guardrails/`](05-the-guardrails/)\n\n---\n\n## The Pitch (M6) · Week of Nov 25\n\n**How you get this funded, shipped, and adopted.**\n\n- **Horizon 1 (Now):**\n- **Horizon 2 (Next):**\n- **Horizon 3 (Bet):**\n- **Board Narrative:** [1-sentence thesis]\n- **Key Metric:**\n\n→ Details: [`06-the-pitch/`](06-the-pitch/)\n\n---\n\n## Open decisions (Week 2)\n\n1. **Partner challenge on Tilt.** Is Tilt the real threat rather than Gusto? It already has AI intake (Tilley), Gusto and Rippling sync, and California coverage. If yes, what does Leave Ledger do that Tilt will not do for a 15-person company?\n2. **Gusto: partner or competitor?** The defense assumes a Gusto app directory listing. Is that the distribution bet, or should the first channel be bookkeepers and PEOs?\n3. **Turn on the Copilot.** Add `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in Netlify and run `scripts/eval-intake.mjs`. The first accuracy score goes into `02-the-moat/kill-switch.md` and feeds Week 4.\n4. **Interviews.** Kill criteria check is Nov 23. Ask each interviewee what they use for payroll; if most say Gusto, that confirms decision 2.\n\n## Changelog\n\n| Week | Date | What changed |\n|------|------|-------------|\n| 1 | Oct 26, 2026 | M1 drafted: market sizing, three-axis diagnostic, prototype bet, kill criteria |\n| 1 | Oct 27, 2026 | Copilot archetype confirmed; interview and partner challenge counters added for the progress page |\n| 1 | Oct 27, 2026 | Copilot intake built (Claude Haiku 5.5): own-words requests fill the form, corrections logged, 20-case golden set added |\n| 2 | Oct 28, 2026 | M2 drafted: flywheel 6/20, competitive map, encroachment threats (Gusto, Tilt, CalChamber), 90-day attack and defense, kill switch audit (portability Partial). Schedule moved to Wednesday sessions |\n","questions.md":"# Weekly decision questions\n\n> Every week's open decisions, kept here so earlier weeks stay answerable on the /strategy page. Each numbered item becomes an answer form there. Format: `1. **Short title.** The question.` The current week also appears under \"Open decisions\" in README.md.\n\n## Week 1 · The Bet\n\n1. **Confirm the archetype.** Copilot with a rules engine underneath, or something else?\n2. **Partner challenge.** Test the three named attackers (Tilt, ChatGPT, Gusto) and the 1/5 data score with your course partner. Record what they push on in `01-the-bet/diagnostic.md`.\n3. **Prototype link.** Add the Netlify URL to `01-the-bet/prototype.md`.\n4. **Start interviews.** Kill criteria 1 and 2 need 10 conversations by Nov 23. If you add 2 to 3 a week, they also feed M2 (data) and M3 (pricing).\n\n## Week 2 · The Moat\n\n1. **Partner challenge on Tilt.** Is Tilt the real threat rather than Gusto? It already has AI intake (Tilley), Gusto and Rippling sync, and California coverage. If yes, what does Leave Ledger do that Tilt will not do for a 15-person company?\n2. **Gusto: partner or competitor?** The defense assumes a Gusto app directory listing. Is that the distribution bet, or should the first channel be bookkeepers and PEOs?\n3. **Turn on the Copilot.** Add `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in Netlify and run `scripts/eval-intake.mjs`. The first accuracy score goes into `02-the-moat/kill-switch.md` and feeds Week 4.\n4. **Interviews.** Kill criteria check is Nov 23. Ask each interviewee what they use for payroll; if most say Gusto, that confirms decision 2.\n"}};
